 "The site included the terminal moraine left by the last ice age (the park's hills, including Lookout Hill) and the wooded Ravine along the Long Meadow's edge.",
 "During the American Revolution (August 1776), the Battle of Long Island was fought across this terrain; the Maryland 400 made their stand near the present-day park's eastern side, commemorated by the Maryland Monument (1895).",
 "Flatbush Woods and nearby settlements (Flatbush, Brooklyn) predated the park; Mount Prospect, just east of the final park site, was Brooklyn's highest point and gave the park its name."
 ]
 },
 {
 "period": "1859–1867 — design and creation",
 "events": [
 "In 1859 Brooklyn, then an independent city (the third largest in the U.S.), began planning a large park; an initial design by Egbert Viele was rejected.",
 "In 1865 Calvert Vaux issued a report criticizing Viele's plan and proposed a new design, joined by Frederick Law Olmsted, fresh from Central Park.",
 "Construction began in 1866 on the Olmsted and Vaux 'Greensward'-style plan; the park officially opened to the public on October 19, 1867, though construction continued for years.",
 "Key design elements: the 90-acre Long Meadow (the longest unbroken meadow in any U.S. park), the 60-acre Ravine and watercourse (Olmsted and Vaux's only stream-and-woodland composition), the 60-acre lake, and the Concert Grove."
 ]
 },
 {
 "period": "1868–1895 — expansion and golden age",
 "events": [
 "Vaux designed the park's bridges and arches (1870s); Olmsted and Vaux's 1868–71 plan added the Parade (now the Parade Ground) at the southern end.",
 "In 1868 Prospect Park West (then Ninth Avenue) and Park Slope development boomed around the park; grand apartment buildings and row houses followed.",
 "Grand Army Plaza (1867–1892), Soldiers' and Sailors' Arch (1892), the Brooklyn Museum (1897) and Brooklyn Botanic Garden (1910) grew up at the park's edges.",
 "Features added: the Camperdown Elm (1872), the Rotunda (1872), the Music Island and Concert Grove pavilion (1887–90)."
 ]
 },
 {
 "period": "1900–1940s — decline and Robert Moses era",
 "events": [
 "The 1898 consolidation of Brooklyn into New York City shifted funding priorities away from the park.",
 "In 1934 Mayor Fiorello La Guardia consolidated parks under Robert Moses, who became Parks Commissioner; Moses-era changes (1934 onward) added the Prospect Park Zoo (opened 1935, rebuilt 1993), the Wollman Rink (1960s), bandshell (1939) and roadways that cut through the Olmsted design.",
 "Mid-century budget shortfalls and heavy use left the meadows, Ravine and woodlands badly eroded."
 ]
 },
 {
 "period": "1964–present — landmark status and restoration",
 "events": [
 "In 1964 the park's original 90-acre Olmsted-and-Vaux-designed core was designated a New York City Scenic Landmark (the Ravine in 1975); the park was added to the National Register of Historic Places in 1966.",
 "In 1980 the Prospect Park Alliance was founded as a public-private partnership with NYC Parks to restore and manage the park.",
 "Major restorations: the Ravine watercourse and Fallkill Falls (1990s–2000s), the Long Meadow erosion repair, the Lakeside project replacing the Wollman Rink with the LeFrak Center (opened 2013), and woodland restoration.",
 "Today the park hosts roughly 8–10 million visits a year and contains the Audubon Center at the Boathouse (opened 2002, the first urban Audubon center in the nation), the Prospect Park Zoo, the Bandshell (Celebrate Brooklyn! concerts), and the last natural forest in Brooklyn."
 ]
 }
 ],
